:pnlgrp.
.************************************************************************
.*  Help for command RCVPIPE
.************************************************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE'.
Receive Record from Unix Cmd - Help
:p.The Receive Record from Unix Cmd (RCVPIPE) command is used in
conjunction with the Open Pipe (OPNPIPE) command. OPNPIPE starts
a QShell or PASE process in the background, and connects it to your
CL program using an interprocess communication object known as a
pipe.
:p.The RCVPIPE command lets you read data from that pipe.
:p.:hp2.Restrictions::ehp2.
:ul.
:li.
You must previously open a pipe with the OPNPIPE command
before you run this.
:li.
See the help for the OPNPIPE command for information about
the authorities needed to use this suite of tools.
:eul.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter RCD
.*******************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE/RCD'.
CL VAR for record (RCD) - Help
:xh3.CL VAR for record (RCD)
:p.Specifies a CL variable that will be used to receive a line
of text data from the connected pipe.
.* Describe the function provided by the parameter.
:p.This is a required parameter.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.
Specify a TYPE(*CHAR) variable that's between 1-32767 bytes long.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter EOF
.*******************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE/EOF'.
CL VAR for End-of-File (EOF) - Help
:xh3.CL VAR for End-of-File (EOF)
:p.Specifies a TYPE(*LGL) variable in which the end-of-file
status can be returned. If a variable is specified here, it
will be set to '0' if data was successfully read from the pipe,
or '1' if the Unix process has ended or specified that there's
no more data to send.
.*
:p.In an effort to make RCVPIPE work similarly to the RCVF command,
if you do not specify the EOF parameter, the RCVPIPE command
will send a CPF0864 *ESCAPE message when end-of-file is reached.
.*
:parml.
:pt.:pv.logical-value:epv.
:pd.
Specify a CL variable to contain the end-of-file indicator.
.*
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter PIPEID
.*******************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE/PIPEID'.
Pipe ID to read from (PIPEID) - Help
:xh3.Pipe ID to read from (PIPEID)
:p.Tells the RCVPIPE which pipe to receive data from. This is
particularly useful if you have more than one pipe open simultaneously.
.*
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*DFT:epk.
:pd.
Read from the default pipe. The default pipe is the pipe that was
most recently opened with the OPNPIPE command.
.*
:pt.:pv.decimal-number:epv.
:pd.
Specify the Pipe ID of an open pipe to receive data from.
.*
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Examples for RCVPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.
Examples for RCVPIPE - Help
:xh3.Examples for RCVPIPE
:p.:hp2.Example 1: Simple Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
RCVPIPE RCD(&LINE)
:exmp.
:p.This command receives one line of data from an open
pipe into a variable named &LINE.
.*
:p.:hp2.More Examples:ehp2.
:p.For more (and better!) examples of the RCVPIPE command,
see the help for the OPNPIPE command.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Error messages for RCVPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='RCVPIPE/ERROR/MESSAGES'.
&msg(CPX0005,QCPFMSG). RCVPIPE - Help
:xh3.&msg(CPX0005,QCPFMSG). RCVPIPE
:p.:hp3.*ESCAPE &msg(CPX0006,QCPFMSG).:ehp3.
.************************************************************************
.* List the *ESCAPE, *STATUS, and *NOTIFY messages signalled from the command.
.* The following are generic messages defined in message file QCPFMSG.
.* Modify this list to match the list of error messages for the command.
.************************************************************************
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF0864
:DD.&MSG(CPF0864,QCPFMSG,*LIBL,nosub).
(This only occurs when the EOF parameter is not specified.)
:DT.CPF9897
:DD.Temporarily, CPF9897 is used to report most messages from
the RCVPIPE command. In a future release of UNIXCMD these
CPF9897 messages will be replaced with messages from a UNIXCMD
message file.
:EDL.
:ehelp.
:epnlgrp.
